{"step_index":114,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T08:38:49Z","content":"I have successfully completed the implementation of all requested changes!\n\nHere is a summary of what has been accomplished:\n\n### 1. Database & SaaS/Desktop Unity\n- **PostgreSQL Default:** The application has been updated to use PostgreSQL by default for both SaaS and Desktop environments (`src/adapters/db/connection.py`). The `psycopg2-binary` driver was also added to your `requirements.txt`.\n- **Composite Indexes:** Added composite indexing `(tenant_id, branch_id)` to all major models (`users`, `patients`, `appointments`, `consultations`, `invoices`) in `orm_models.py` to ensure scalable multi-tenant performance without maintaining separate codebases.\n\n### 2. The `Doctor_All` Role\n- Added `DOCTOR_ALL` to the `UserRole` enum.\n- Updated `router_queue.py` and `dependencies.py`:\n  - `DOCTOR_ALL` now dynamically bypasses the standard doctor visibility constraints. This role can view the entire clinic's live queue (just like an Admin) but also retains the clinical write privileges (like a standard Doctor) to start consultations and edit records for any patient.\n\n### 3. Documentation\n- Completely rewrote **`README.md`** to reflect the unified SaaS/Desktop architecture, PostgreSQL requirement, and updated RBAC roles.\n- Created a new **`USER_GUIDE.md`** detailing step-by-step instructions for Admins, Receptionists, and Doctors on how to use the Registration, Queue, and Consultancy modules.\n\n---\n\n### Proposal for SMS / Email Gateways (Phase 2 Preparation)\nFor your upcoming notification integrations (appointment reminders, digital prescriptions), here are the most efficient options tailored for a clinic ERP:\n\n#### A. SMS & WhatsApp Options\n1. **Twilio (Global standard)**\n   - *Pros:* Highly reliable, robust API, excellent documentation, supports both programmable SMS and the WhatsApp Business API.\n   - *Cons:* Slightly more expensive per message, especially outside the US.\n2. **MessageBird / Sinch**\n   - *Pros:* Often cheaper than Twilio for international routing. Excellent multi-channel support (SMS + WhatsApp).\n3. **Local Providers (e.g., Textlocal, Gupshup - if operating in India/Asia)**\n   - *Pros:* Significantly cheaper for domestic messages. Fast approval for DLT (Distributed Ledger Technology) templates required for transactional clinic SMS in regions like India.\n   - *Cons:* APIs are sometimes less modern than Twilio.\n\n#### B. Email Options\n1. **SendGrid (by Twilio) / Mailgun**\n   - *Pros:* Industry standards for transactional emails (invoices, welcome emails). Free tiers are very generous (e.g., SendGrid offers 100 emails/day free forever), making it perfect for small clinics.\n2. **Amazon SES**\n   - *Pros:* The absolute cheapest option available ($0.10 per 1000 emails).\n   - *Cons:* Requires AWS account setup and domain verification, which has a slightly steeper learning curve.\n\n**My Recommendation:** Start with **SendGrid** for emails (due to the generous free tier) and **Twilio** (if global) or **Gupshup** (if Asia-focused) for SMS/WhatsApp. We can build an abstract `NotificationService` in Phase 2 so you can swap providers easily without rewriting core logic.\n\nEverything is pushed and ready! Let me know if you would like to test the new `DOCTOR_ALL` permissions or if you want to proceed with any UI theme enhancements."}
