{"step_index":137,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-30T11:49:45Z","content":"This is an excellent question! Because we just transitioned your application to a unified **FastAPI + PostgreSQL** architecture, **you actually do not need to make any code changes to support a multi-PC LAN setup!** \n\nThe system is already inherently designed as a client-server web application. Here is exactly how the hardware should be set up and configured for a clinic operating on a local LAN.\n\n### 1. Hardware Setup (Clinic Side)\n\nYou do not need an expensive, dedicated server rack. You just need to designate one of the existing PCs as the \"Server.\"\n\n*   **The \"Server\" PC (e.g., The Reception Desk PC):**\n    *   **Specs:** A standard modern PC (Core i5/Ryzen 5, 8GB+ RAM, SSD) is more than enough.\n    *   **Role:** This computer will host the PostgreSQL database and run the FastAPI Python server (via `start_server.bat` or Docker). \n    *   **Usage:** The receptionist can use this PC normally for billing and appointments while the server runs silently in the background.\n*   **The \"Client\" PC (e.g., The Doctor's PC):**\n    *   **Specs:** Any basic computer or even a tablet.\n    *   **Role:** Does not run any databases or Python scripts. It only needs a modern web browser (Chrome, Edge, Safari).\n*   **Networking:** Both PCs must simply be connected to the same clinic Wi-Fi or wired network switch.\n\n### 2. Application & Configuration Changes\n\nAs mentioned, **zero code changes** are required. However, the person installing the software at the clinic must perform the following configuration steps:\n\n#### A. Bind to the Network Interface (Already Done!)\nFor the Doctor's PC to reach the Receptionist's PC, the server must listen on the local network, not just \"localhost.\" \n*   If you look at the `start_server.bat` file we updated earlier, it already includes `--host 0.0.0.0`. This tells the application to accept connections from any PC on the network.\n\n#### B. Configure Windows Firewall (Crucial Step)\nBy default, Windows blocks incoming network requests. On the **Server PC**, you must create a firewall rule:\n1. Open **Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security**.\n2. Click **Inbound Rules** -> **New Rule**.\n3. Select **Port** -> **TCP** -> Specific local ports: **8000**.\n4. Allow the connection and name it \"Clinic ERP\".\n*(Note: You only need to open port 8000 for the web UI. You do not need to expose port 5432 for Postgres, keeping the database secure from other network devices).*\n\n#### C. Finding the IP Address\n1. On the **Server PC**, open Command Prompt and type `ipconfig`.\n2. Look for the **IPv4 Address** (e.g., `192.168.1.100`).\n3. It is highly recommended to set this Server PC to a **Static IP** in the router settings so the address doesn't change when the router reboots.\n\n#### D. Accessing the App from the Doctor's PC\nThe doctor simply opens Google Chrome on their PC and types the Server PC's IP address followed by the port:\n```text\nhttp://192.168.1.100:8000/ui/\n```\n*(You can create a desktop shortcut on the Doctor's PC that points to this URL so it feels like a native desktop app).*\n\n### Summary\nBecause you chose a unified architecture, your \"Desktop\" version operates exactly like a mini, localized SaaS. The database handles the concurrent writes from both the Doctor and the Receptionist flawlessly, and no data synchronization logic is required!","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm now focusing on tool selection, understanding that choosing the right tool is key. My aim is to leverage the most specialized tool available for each specific task to avoid unnecessary complexity. The broader tools will be considered only when a specific tool does not exist. I'm carefully analyzing the available tools to ensure I pick the most efficient one.\n\n\n**Refining Deployment Strategy**\n\nI'm now refining the deployment strategy for the LAN-based clinic application. After recalling critical instructions, I am focusing on minimizing tool complexity. My thought process includes explicitly listing available tools before execution. I am trying to determine the most effective method for deploying the application across multiple PCs, for roles like receptionist and doctor, in a LAN setting. I'm focusing on minimizing the need for complex tool chains.\n\n\n**Defining Hardware & Changes**\n\nI'm now determining hardware setup, and required application changes for the clinic. A dedicated server PC with PostgreSQL and FastAPI is needed, with standard client PCs running a modern web browser. Thankfully, no code changes are necessary, just configuration tweaks to bind the FastAPI server.\n\n\n"}
